EXPERIMENT 9: Use Secrets in GitHub Actions Securely and Build a Docker Image

AIM:
To securely use a GitHub repository secret in a GitHub Actions workflow and automatically build a Docker image when code is pushed to the main branch.

PROCEDURE:

STEP 1: Open CMD and navigate to the cloned repository.

cd "%USERPROFILE%\GitExperiment1\GitExperiment1"

STEP 2: Check the current Git status.

git status

STEP 3: Create the Dockerfile using CMD.

(echo FROM nginx:alpine&echo COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html) > Dockerfile

STEP 4: Verify that Dockerfile and index.html exist.

dir Dockerfile
dir index.html

STEP 5: Create the GitHub Actions workflow folder if it does not exist.

mkdir .github\workflows

If CMD says the folder already exists, continue.

STEP 6: Open Notepad to create the workflow file.

notepad .github\workflows\docker-build.yml

Paste the following YAML into Notepad, then save and close it.

-------------------- docker-build.yml --------------------

name: Build Docker Image

on:
  push:
    branches:
      - main

jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:
      - name: Checkout repository
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Check repository secret securely
        env:
          API_KEY: ${{ secrets.API_KEY }}
        run: |
          if [ -z "$API_KEY" ]; then
            echo "API_KEY secret is missing"
            exit 1
          else
            echo "API_KEY secret is available"
          fi

      - name: Build Docker Image
        run: docker build -t my-app-image .

-------------------- END OF YAML --------------------

STEP 7: Add the repository secret on GitHub.

1. Open the GitHub repository.
2. Go to Settings.
3. Select Secrets and variables.
4. Select Actions.
5. Click New repository secret.
6. Enter the secret name: API_KEY
7. Enter a demo/test value as the secret value.
8. Click Add secret.

Do not print or display the secret value in the workflow logs.

If API_KEY already exists from the previous experiment, use the existing secret.

STEP 8: Stage the required files.

git add index.html Dockerfile .github\workflows\docker-build.yml

STEP 9: Check the staged files.

git status

The following files should be staged:
- index.html
- Dockerfile
- .github/workflows/docker-build.yml

If Dockerfile.txt appears as an untracked file, remove the accidental text file:

del Dockerfile.txt

Then check again:

git status

STEP 10: Commit the changes.

git commit -m "Add Docker image build workflow"

STEP 11: Push the changes to GitHub.

git push origin main

STEP 12: Verify the GitHub Actions workflow.

1. Open the GitHub repository.
2. Click the Actions tab.
3. Open the workflow named Build Docker Image.
4. Check the workflow run status.
5. A green Success status means the workflow completed successfully.

RESULT:
The GitHub Actions workflow successfully checked that the repository secret was available and built a Docker image automatically after pushing changes to the main branch.
